.TH AWK 1
.SH NAME
awk \- pattern-action language, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B awk
[
.B -F
.I fs
]
[
.B -v
.I var=value
]
[
.B -f
.I progfile
|
.I 'program'
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Awk
is a Limbo-native workalike for a bounded subset of the AWK language,
compiled to Dis and run by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for why that distinction is the point of this
command's existing at all.
It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ,
kept out of the regular userspace on purpose; see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
For each
.I file
(standard input if none are given),
.I awk
splits each line into fields on
.I fs
(a single literal character, or plain whitespace \(em runs collapsed,
leading/trailing ignored \(em by default) and, for each pattern-action
pair in
.I program
whose pattern matches, executes the action.
A missing action defaults to printing the whole line; a missing
pattern always matches.
.B BEGIN
and
.B END
actions run once, before the first line and after the last.
.PP
.B -F
sets the field separator.
.B -v
assigns a variable before
.B BEGIN
runs.
.B -f
reads the program from
.I progfile
instead of the command line (repeatable; the files are concatenated).
.SS Language covered
Patterns:
.BR BEGIN ,
.BR END ,
.BI / regex /,
any expression (true if non-zero/non-empty), and the empty pattern.
Statements:
.BR print ,
.BR printf ,
.BR if / else ,
.BR while ,
C-style
.BR for (\c
.IR init ; cond ; post ),
.BI for( "k in arr" ")",
.BR next ,
blocks, and expression statements.
Expressions: the usual arithmetic, comparison, and logical operators;
string concatenation by juxtaposition;
.B ~
and
.B !~
regex match; ternary
.BR ?: ;
.BR ++ / -- ,
prefix and postfix; assignment and
.BR += / -= / *= / /= / %= ;
.B $
field reference; associative arrays.
Builtins:
.BR length ,
.BR substr ,
.BR index ,
.BR split ,
.BR sprintf ,
.BR toupper ,
.BR tolower ,
.BR int .
Special variables:
.BR NR ,
.BR NF ,
.BR FS ,
.BR OFS ,
.BR ORS ,
.BR FILENAME .
.B printf
honors width, left-align
.RB ( - ),
zero-pad
.RB ( 0 ),
and precision, for
.BR %s / %d / %i / %f / %g / %e / %c / %% .
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/awk.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR awktest (1),
.B doc/plan9-port.md
.SH BUGS
This is a deliberately bounded subset, not the full POSIX grammar, and
every gap below is a scoping choice made to keep the interpreter a
single readable file, not an oversight found later:
.PP
No user-defined functions
.RB ( function
.IR name ( args )
.BR { ... } ),
and no
.B getline.
No
.BR break / continue
(loops run to completion or exit via
.BR next ).
No output redirection
.RB ( print
.B >
.IR file ),
so
.B print
and
.B printf
always take a bare, comma-separated argument list \(em
.B print(a,b)
with enclosing parens fails to parse; write
.B print a, b
instead.
.PP
A bare newline is never a statement terminator, unlike real awk;
statements must be
.B ;
separated (a block's closing
.B }
ends the last one without a semicolon).
.PP
.B FS
longer than one character is matched as a literal substring, not a
regular expression.
.PP
.B printf
precision is clamped to 0\(en6 digits for
.BR %f / %g / %e ,
since Limbo's
.B sys->sprint
requires its format argument to be a compile-time string constant \(em
a precision parsed from the awk program at run time can't be handed to
it directly, so the conversion goes through one of a fixed set of
literal format strings keyed on the parsed precision instead of a
freshly built one.
.PP
A side-effecting index or field-number expression \(em
.B arr[i++]
or
.BR $(i++) \(em
evaluates that subexpression twice, once to read the current value and
once to write the new one.
.PP
Associative-array enumeration order
.RB ( for
.RI ( k " in " arr ))
is unspecified, matching POSIX awk \(em
.IR awktest (1)
checks it as a set, not as an exact sequence.
.PP
Correctness for what
.I is
covered is gated on matching
.BR utils/awk 's
own C implementation, byte for byte, on the same program and input \(em
not on this description, and not on the POSIX text from memory.
.PP
No regex-cache: every
.BI / regex /
literal and
.B ~
match recompiles its pattern. Fine at the scale this was built for;
worth revisiting first if performance ever matters here.
